abstract: >-
  HDBLAST investigates whether a higher-dimensional gravitational event could have supplied
  the hot initial state of our universe. Its five-dimensional Einstein–scalar shell model
  has a public record of exact mathematics, reproducible calculations, scoped error bounds,
  negative results and corrections. The 8 October 2026 checkpoint encloses all 98,304 retained
  later-endpoint discrepancies relative to exact prescribed flow from the represented saved
  incoming state; this does not recover the unsaved dense path. Separate conditional flat-bulk
  response and incident-packet models have an exact four-dimensional continuum replica and
  establish no unique dimensional inference. The 11 October checkpoint proves local
  scalar-junction shell-matching monotonicity and extends the registered exact-decimal
  regular-branch certificate to every 0 < delta <= 0.002, with uniform scalar and curvature
  remainder constants below 3.196 and 0.254, respectively. Curvature is strictly below that
  of the separately specified complete boundary theory with scalar-independent detuning.
  The 13 October checkpoint proves positive scalar spectral bounds at each fixed positive
  detuning in this interval for the specified even and odd finite-energy radial operators,
  completes the local moving-shell kinetic calculation, and establishes the nonzero
  transverse-traceless tensor mass threshold 3H_b/2. The regular branch near phi=+1 is
  distinct from the original unstable hilltop branch of the same action; no evolution
  connecting them is proved. Global gauge admissibility, cone symplectic flux and physical
  state-space completeness remain conditional. These are mathematical and local-action
  results, not a full dynamical-stability theorem. The
  continuous pressure/contact/time/momentum/ultraviolet certificate remains unresolved,
  prior metric calibration remains failed, and a higher-dimensional Big Bang cause,
  reheating and observational confirmation remain unestablished. Internal AI-assisted
  reviews and independent computational checks are not external peer review.
